1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:04,536
For five minutes, you've been chatting with two people.

2
00:00:04,556 --> 00:00:07,347
Both of their replies seem completely normal.

3
00:00:07,367 --> 00:00:12,283
Sometimes a bit of small talk, sometimes a joke, sometimes a personal question.

4
00:00:12,303 --> 00:00:14,157
But only one of them is human.

5
00:00:14,177 --> 00:00:15,344
The other is an AI.

6
00:00:15,364 --> 00:00:18,280
Now you have to decide which one is the person.

7
00:00:18,950 --> 00:00:24,417
In one experiment, when GPT-4.5 was told to talk like an ordinary young person,

8
00:00:24,437 --> 00:00:28,028
people took it for the human 73 percent of the time.

9
00:00:28,048 --> 00:00:30,181
And here's the surprising part.

10
00:00:30,201 --> 00:00:35,390
When those special instructions were removed, that rate fell to 36 percent.

11
00:00:36,060 --> 00:00:39,211
In other words, it wasn't just the machine's answers.

12
00:00:39,231 --> 00:00:42,083
Its way of talking was shaping our decision too.

13
00:00:42,103 --> 00:00:45,075
But there's a difference here that's easy to miss.

14
00:00:45,095 --> 00:00:48,964
Taking a machine for a human, and that machine actually thinking,

15
00:00:48,984 --> 00:00:50,340
are not the same thing.

16
00:00:51,010 --> 00:00:54,496
So how do we test whether a machine is only imitating well,

17
00:00:54,516 --> 00:00:56,457
whether it understands something,

18
00:00:56,477 --> 00:00:59,904
or whether anything is really being experienced inside it?

19
00:00:59,924 --> 00:01:03,054
Searching for an answer, we'll travel to an old idea,

20
00:01:03,074 --> 00:01:06,560
an extraordinary move in the game of Go, and a locked room.

21
00:01:07,930 --> 00:01:11,131
In 1950, the mathematician Alan Turing wrote a paper.

22
00:01:11,151 --> 00:01:14,352
It begins with a simple question: can machines think?

23
00:01:14,372 --> 00:01:19,457
But he quickly realises that first we'd have to agree on what "thinking" even means.

24
00:01:19,477 --> 00:01:22,800
And people could argue about that definition for years.

25
00:01:23,470 --> 00:01:25,445
So Turing takes a different route.

26
00:01:25,465 --> 00:01:29,728
Imagine a person who talks with two others only through written messages.

27
00:01:29,748 --> 00:01:34,304
On the other side are a human and a machine, but he isn't told which is which.

28
00:01:34,324 --> 00:01:38,000
After the conversation, he has to guess which one is the human.

29
00:01:38,670 --> 00:01:41,008
Today we call this idea the Turing test.

30
00:01:41,028 --> 00:01:43,719
It isn't a test of looking inside the machine.

31
00:01:43,739 --> 00:01:47,256
It judges its behaviour from the outside: in a conversation,

32
00:01:47,276 --> 00:01:49,260
can we tell it apart from a human?

33
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:51,598
That had a real advantage.

34
00:01:51,618 --> 00:01:55,858
Instead of fighting over a definition of thinking that nobody agrees on,

35
00:01:55,878 --> 00:01:58,816
you could ask a question you can actually observe.

36
00:01:58,836 --> 00:02:02,070
But that is also exactly where the test's limit begins.

37
00:02:02,090 --> 00:02:05,560
Sounding human is not proof of understanding on the inside.

38
00:02:06,230 --> 00:02:08,455
Think back to a viva, the spoken exam.

39
00:02:08,475 --> 00:02:12,354
If a student gives the right answer, the teacher may be satisfied.

40
00:02:12,374 --> 00:02:16,785
But sometimes the very next question reveals that the answer was memorised,

41
00:02:16,805 --> 00:02:17,671
not understood.

42
00:02:17,691 --> 00:02:20,920
With machines, this same difference is going to matter.

43
00:02:22,290 --> 00:02:25,492
The roots of this doubt go back even before Turing.

44
00:02:25,512 --> 00:02:29,852
In 1843, Ada Lovelace wrote about Charles Babbage's proposed machine,

45
00:02:29,872 --> 00:02:31,242
the Analytical Engine.

46
00:02:31,262 --> 00:02:35,096
It was a plan for a kind of computer that was never finished,

47
00:02:35,116 --> 00:02:37,560
but its possibilities were astonishing.

48
00:02:38,230 --> 00:02:43,156
Ada added an important word of caution: the machine makes no claim to create

49
00:02:43,176 --> 00:02:44,718
anything new on its own.

50
00:02:44,738 --> 00:02:47,907
It can only do what we know how to tell it to do.

51
00:02:47,927 --> 00:02:52,137
You still hear this objection today, that a computer, in the end,

52
00:02:52,157 --> 00:02:54,480
only runs the rules a human gave it.

53
00:02:55,150 --> 00:02:57,242
Turing took this objection seriously.

54
00:02:57,262 --> 00:03:01,809
Part of his answer was that we don't need to write out every possible answer for

55
00:03:01,829 --> 00:03:03,122
the machine in advance.

56
00:03:03,142 --> 00:03:06,832
Why not build a machine that learns like a child, makes mistakes,

57
00:03:06,852 --> 00:03:09,230
and changes its behaviour with experience?

58
00:03:10,050 --> 00:03:13,952
Today that idea sounds ordinary, but it changes the whole debate.

59
00:03:13,972 --> 00:03:17,573
The question is no longer which answer the programmer wrote.

60
00:03:17,593 --> 00:03:20,711
The question becomes what a machine, after learning,

61
00:03:20,731 --> 00:03:24,030
can do that even the people who built it didn't expect.

62
00:03:24,700 --> 00:03:26,078
And then, during a game,

63
00:03:26,098 --> 00:03:30,270
a moment came that made some of the world's best experts stop and stare.

64
00:03:30,290 --> 00:03:34,579
But to understand it, we first need to see the difference between two very

65
00:03:34,599 --> 00:03:36,210
different kinds of computer.

66
00:03:37,580 --> 00:03:41,873
In 1997, IBM's computer Deep Blue defeated the world chess champion,

67
00:03:41,893 --> 00:03:43,585
Garry Kasparov, in a match.

68
00:03:43,605 --> 00:03:48,849
It checked possible moves at enormous speed and weighed up what each one would gain

69
00:03:48,869 --> 00:03:49,357
or lose.

70
00:03:49,377 --> 00:03:51,830
It was a huge achievement in computing.

71
00:03:52,430 --> 00:03:55,537
In 2016, the contest reached a different game: Go.

72
00:03:55,557 --> 00:04:00,478
Lee Sedol, one of South Korea's greatest players, sat down across from AlphaGo,

73
00:04:00,498 --> 00:04:02,604
a system built by Google DeepMind.

74
00:04:02,624 --> 00:04:07,545
On Go's simple-looking board there are so many possibilities that judging every

75
00:04:07,565 --> 00:04:11,110
move just by counting them all is practically impossible.

76
00:04:11,780 --> 00:04:14,486
AlphaGo learned from games played by expert humans,

77
00:04:14,506 --> 00:04:18,333
and then kept improving by playing huge numbers of games against itself.

78
00:04:18,353 --> 00:04:21,861
So it wasn't just reading from a list of moves written in advance.

79
00:04:21,881 --> 00:04:25,976
Through experience, it built up a sense of which decision works best in which

80
00:04:25,996 --> 00:04:26,510
situation.

81
00:04:27,180 --> 00:04:30,666
In the second game, AlphaGo played its thirty-seventh move.

82
00:04:30,686 --> 00:04:32,745
Today it's remembered as "Move 37".

83
00:04:32,765 --> 00:04:37,618
The move was so unusual that the commentators couldn't immediately tell whether it

84
00:04:37,638 --> 00:04:40,470
was a mistake, or part of some far-sighted plan.

85
00:04:41,140 --> 00:04:44,464
According to DeepMind, judging by the data from human games,

86
00:04:44,484 --> 00:04:48,366
the chance of a human playing that move was about one in ten thousand.

87
00:04:48,386 --> 00:04:51,766
But as the game went on, the move's importance began to show.

88
00:04:51,786 --> 00:04:56,170
In the end, AlphaGo won that game, and took the whole series four games to one.

89
00:04:56,840 --> 00:05:00,256
This moment isn't memorable because a machine beat a human.

90
00:05:00,276 --> 00:05:04,856
What matters is that a learning system chose a strategy that was different from

91
00:05:04,876 --> 00:05:08,000
both the habits and the expectations of human experts.

92
00:05:08,670 --> 00:05:11,138
So, is Ada Lovelace's objection settled?

93
00:05:11,158 --> 00:05:12,071
Not completely.

94
00:05:12,091 --> 00:05:17,110
It did show that machines can produce results we never wrote out for them step by

95
00:05:17,130 --> 00:05:17,421
step.

96
00:05:17,441 --> 00:05:22,460
But playing a new and winning move is not final proof of understanding that move.

97
00:05:23,830 --> 00:05:28,969
In 1980, the philosopher John Searle explained this same objection with a memorable

98
00:05:28,989 --> 00:05:29,466
example.

99
00:05:29,486 --> 00:05:31,704
Imagine you're locked inside a room.

100
00:05:31,724 --> 00:05:34,190
You don't know a single word of Chinese.

101
00:05:34,210 --> 00:05:37,920
To you, sentences written in Chinese are just strange marks.

102
00:05:38,590 --> 00:05:40,916
A slip of paper comes in under the door.

103
00:05:40,936 --> 00:05:43,144
Something is written on it in Chinese.

104
00:05:43,164 --> 00:05:47,073
You have a thick book of instructions: if you see marks like these,

105
00:05:47,093 --> 00:05:49,770
write these marks in reply and pass them back.

106
00:05:50,440 --> 00:05:53,137
You follow the book carefully, step by step.

107
00:05:53,157 --> 00:05:58,077
Outside, the Chinese speakers get such fitting replies that they believe someone

108
00:05:58,097 --> 00:05:59,930
inside the room knows Chinese.

109
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:02,645
But nothing about you has changed.

110
00:06:02,665 --> 00:06:07,139
You don't know what the incoming note means, or what your own reply means.

111
00:06:07,159 --> 00:06:11,512
You followed the rules perfectly, but you never understood the language.

112
00:06:11,532 --> 00:06:16,310
That was Searle's question: is arranging symbols according to rules the same as

113
00:06:16,330 --> 00:06:17,160
understanding?

114
00:06:17,980 --> 00:06:19,503
One famous reply came back.

115
00:06:19,523 --> 00:06:22,474
Maybe the person inside the room doesn't understand,

116
00:06:22,494 --> 00:06:25,846
but the whole system does: the person, the book, the rules,

117
00:06:25,866 --> 00:06:27,731
and all of them working together.

118
00:06:27,751 --> 00:06:28,703
Searle disagreed.

119
00:06:28,723 --> 00:06:32,131
He said that even if the person memorised every instruction,

120
00:06:32,151 --> 00:06:34,760
he still wouldn't know what the Chinese means.

121
00:06:35,430 --> 00:06:39,612
This debate still hasn't fully ended, and neither side can settle it overnight.

122
00:06:39,632 --> 00:06:43,974
But Searle's example at least reminds us that there can be a gap between an answer

123
00:06:43,994 --> 00:06:46,580
that looks right and understanding on the inside.

124
00:06:47,250 --> 00:06:49,121
But now a new question comes up.

125
00:06:49,141 --> 00:06:53,552
Are today's AI systems also just repeating the rules of some enormous book?

126
00:06:53,572 --> 00:06:58,161
Or, while they learn, does some other way of organising information take shape

127
00:06:58,181 --> 00:06:58,870
inside them?

128
00:07:00,240 --> 00:07:02,928
To test this, researchers turned to a small game.

129
00:07:02,948 --> 00:07:04,420
The game is called Othello.

130
00:07:04,440 --> 00:07:08,288
Black and white pieces are placed on a board, and with every new move,

131
00:07:08,308 --> 00:07:09,670
the pieces keep changing.

132
00:07:10,340 --> 00:07:14,865
The researchers trained a small GPT-like model on sequences of moves from games.

133
00:07:14,885 --> 00:07:17,251
It was never shown a picture of the board,

134
00:07:17,271 --> 00:07:20,318
and it wasn't taught the rules of the game separately.

135
00:07:20,338 --> 00:07:22,420
All it had was the sequence of moves,

136
00:07:22,440 --> 00:07:25,090
and its job was to predict the next legal move.

137
00:07:25,760 --> 00:07:29,701
After plenty of practice, the model got very good at its predictions.

138
00:07:29,721 --> 00:07:34,122
Now the real question was this: was it just memorising common move sequences,

139
00:07:34,142 --> 00:07:38,600
or did it somehow also know where the pieces were on the board at that moment?

140
00:07:39,420 --> 00:07:42,135
The researchers examined its internal activity.

141
00:07:42,155 --> 00:07:46,674
They found evidence that something like a map of the current board was forming

142
00:07:46,694 --> 00:07:47,663
inside the model.

143
00:07:47,683 --> 00:07:52,260
Information about where each piece might be was there, inside its calculations.

144
00:07:52,930 --> 00:07:54,444
Then they ran another test.

145
00:07:54,464 --> 00:07:58,703
They artificially changed the model's internal information about the board,

146
00:07:58,723 --> 00:08:00,634
as if one square had been flipped.

147
00:08:00,654 --> 00:08:04,496
As a result, the model's predictions for the next moves changed too,

148
00:08:04,516 --> 00:08:06,030
to match the altered board.

149
00:08:06,700 --> 00:08:11,554
That strengthened the case that the internal map wasn't just some unimportant side

150
00:08:11,574 --> 00:08:11,970
effect.

151
00:08:11,990 --> 00:08:14,883
The model was using it to make its next decision.

152
00:08:14,903 --> 00:08:16,428
In a small, limited world,

153
00:08:16,448 --> 00:08:21,064
it had learned to organise information in a way nobody taught it directly from

154
00:08:21,084 --> 00:08:21,540
outside.

155
00:08:22,210 --> 00:08:24,005
But caution is needed here.

156
00:08:24,025 --> 00:08:26,964
A small Othello board is not the real world.

157
00:08:26,984 --> 00:08:32,209
And evidence of an internal map is not proof of full, humanlike understanding.

158
00:08:32,229 --> 00:08:37,588
Still, this result does weaken the simple picture of a machine that only repeats

159
00:08:37,608 --> 00:08:39,000
ready-made sentences.

160
00:08:40,370 --> 00:08:45,549
On the other hand, some of the mistakes large language models make are interesting

161
00:08:45,569 --> 00:08:45,802
too.

162
00:08:45,822 --> 00:08:50,494
In a 2023 study, researchers found that a model could state a relationship

163
00:08:50,514 --> 00:08:52,206
correctly in one direction,

164
00:08:52,226 --> 00:08:56,580
yet fail to recognise the very same relationship the other way round.

165
00:08:57,250 --> 00:09:01,750
For example, it may know that Tom Cruise's mother is Mary Lee Pfeiffer.

166
00:09:01,770 --> 00:09:06,843
But when asked who Mary Lee Pfeiffer's son is, it may not give the right answer.

167
00:09:06,863 --> 00:09:09,135
Of course, people forget things too.

168
00:09:09,155 --> 00:09:14,356
But a systematic mistake like this makes us ask how the model actually learned the

169
00:09:14,376 --> 00:09:15,120
information.

170
00:09:15,790 --> 00:09:18,728
This problem was named the "reversal curse".

171
00:09:18,748 --> 00:09:24,240
In related tests, GPT-4 answered questions about celebrities' parents correctly 79

172
00:09:24,260 --> 00:09:26,727
percent of the time in one direction,

173
00:09:26,747 --> 00:09:30,827
but in the reverse direction its accuracy fell to 33 percent.

174
00:09:30,847 --> 00:09:34,323
This was a specific test of a model checked in 2023.

175
00:09:34,343 --> 00:09:38,020
It shouldn't be taken as a permanent limit of every AI.

176
00:09:38,690 --> 00:09:40,916
Now we have both sides of the picture.

177
00:09:40,936 --> 00:09:45,113
In some places, the machine seems to build a working map inside itself,

178
00:09:45,133 --> 00:09:48,305
and in others, a simple reversed question confuses it.

179
00:09:48,325 --> 00:09:53,094
That's why quick verdicts like "it understands" or "it doesn't understand at all"

180
00:09:53,114 --> 00:09:55,340
can go further than the evidence does.

181
00:09:56,710 --> 00:09:59,766
Now let's go back to the experiment we started with.

182
00:09:59,786 --> 00:10:01,895
The research first appeared in 2025,

183
00:10:01,915 --> 00:10:05,267
and was published in 2026 in the scientific journal PNAS.

184
00:10:05,287 --> 00:10:10,059
In the experiment, the person asking questions held a written conversation with a

185
00:10:10,079 --> 00:10:12,011
human and an AI at the same time.

186
00:10:12,031 --> 00:10:14,850
Then they had to decide which one was the human.

187
00:10:15,520 --> 00:10:20,483
When GPT-4.5 was asked to take on the style of a somewhat shy young person who

188
00:10:20,503 --> 00:10:25,084
knows internet slang, it was chosen as the human 73 percent of the time.

189
00:10:25,104 --> 00:10:30,003
Without that special instruction to adopt the style, the rate was 36 percent.

190
00:10:30,023 --> 00:10:33,900
That's why this result reflects not only the model's ability,

191
00:10:33,920 --> 00:10:37,670
but also the design of the experiment and its instructions.

192
00:10:38,340 --> 00:10:42,868
The researchers also extended the conversations from five minutes to fifteen.

193
00:10:42,888 --> 00:10:46,765
In that separate test, with the same kind of persona instructions,

194
00:10:46,785 --> 00:10:50,250
GPT-5 was taken for the human about 59 percent of the time.

195
00:10:50,270 --> 00:10:52,317
In other words, in some conditions,

196
00:10:52,337 --> 00:10:56,510
even more time didn't make it easier for people to tell the difference.

197
00:10:57,180 --> 00:10:59,065
When people made their decisions,

198
00:10:59,085 --> 00:11:02,125
they often paid attention to cues like writing style,

199
00:11:02,145 --> 00:11:04,723
the flow of the conversation and personality,

200
00:11:04,743 --> 00:11:07,610
rather than deep knowledge or difficult reasoning.

201
00:11:07,630 --> 00:11:11,420
Sometimes even a small mistake could make a reply seem more human.

202
00:11:12,090 --> 00:11:16,853
So the strong conclusion from this experiment is that some AI systems can imitate a

203
00:11:16,873 --> 00:11:19,618
human very convincingly in a short conversation.

204
00:11:19,638 --> 00:11:24,228
But on its own, this experiment doesn't tell us how much they understand inside,

205
00:11:24,248 --> 00:11:26,360
or whether they feel anything at all.

206
00:11:27,710 --> 00:11:31,808
Having come this far, perhaps the hardest distinction comes into view.

207
00:11:31,828 --> 00:11:33,632
Solving a problem is one thing.

208
00:11:33,652 --> 00:11:36,927
Using information about the world around you is another.

209
00:11:36,947 --> 00:11:41,634
And feeling something from the inside, having a personal experience, is a third.

210
00:11:41,654 --> 00:11:45,340
These three questions are connected, but they are not the same.

211
00:11:46,010 --> 00:11:49,418
For example, an AI can write a beautiful poem about rain.

212
00:11:49,438 --> 00:11:54,471
It can say true things about the clouds, the smell of wet earth and the cool breeze.

213
00:11:54,491 --> 00:11:58,260
But that doesn't automatically prove it has ever felt the rain.

214
00:11:58,930 --> 00:12:03,488
In 2023, nineteen researchers tried to examine the question of consciousness in AI

215
00:12:03,508 --> 00:12:04,884
in a more systematic way.

216
00:12:04,904 --> 00:12:09,463
They drew a set of indicators from different scientific theories of consciousness,

217
00:12:09,483 --> 00:12:12,980
and then looked at where such indicators show up in AI systems.

218
00:12:13,650 --> 00:12:15,250
According to that assessment,

219
00:12:15,270 --> 00:12:19,664
there was no convincing evidence that the systems they studied at the time were

220
00:12:19,684 --> 00:12:20,222
conscious.

221
00:12:20,242 --> 00:12:24,636
But the researchers also said that building systems with such indicators in the

222
00:12:24,656 --> 00:12:27,094
future doesn't seem impossible in principle.

223
00:12:27,114 --> 00:12:31,340
And even finding the indicators would not automatically prove consciousness.

224
00:12:32,010 --> 00:12:36,961
So the debate is no longer only about how well a computer can hold a conversation.

225
00:12:36,981 --> 00:12:41,932
The question is also what a reliable way to recognise consciousness would even be.

226
00:12:41,952 --> 00:12:46,236
When we can't directly see the inner experience of another human being,

227
00:12:46,256 --> 00:12:48,540
judging a machine becomes even harder.

228
00:12:49,910 --> 00:12:51,395
So, can machines think?

229
00:12:51,415 --> 00:12:56,826
Today, instead of answering in a single word, we can ask the question more clearly.

230
00:12:56,846 --> 00:12:59,705
Can a machine hold a humanlike conversation?

231
00:12:59,725 --> 00:13:01,341
In some experiments, yes.

232
00:13:01,361 --> 00:13:05,529
Can it learn, and build a useful map of the world inside itself?

233
00:13:05,549 --> 00:13:08,670
In limited tasks, there is evidence that it can.

234
00:13:09,340 --> 00:13:13,755
Does all this mean it understands the way we do, or that it feels anything?

235
00:13:13,775 --> 00:13:16,358
On that, there is no decisive agreement yet.

236
00:13:16,378 --> 00:13:20,852
Perhaps the better way forward is this: we shouldn't play down what machines

237
00:13:20,872 --> 00:13:25,583
achieve, but we also shouldn't draw conclusions from it that we have no evidence

238
00:13:25,603 --> 00:13:25,820
for.

239
00:13:26,490 --> 00:13:28,814
And now, the last question is for you.

240
00:13:28,834 --> 00:13:33,625
Imagine a machine that talks with you exactly like a human, learns new things,

241
00:13:33,645 --> 00:13:35,784
and even corrects its own mistakes.

242
00:13:35,804 --> 00:13:40,040
To you, what would be the strongest proof that it is really thinking?
